│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ shared_params_deep_entangle                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ Radically reduce parameter count through aggressive sharing while increasing              │
│                          │ entanglement:                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Replace 8 independent RY gates with 2 shared RY parameters (even/odd qubits)           │
│                          │ 2. Replace 16 independent RZ gates with 2 shared RZ parameters (pre/post layers, all      │
│                          │ qubits)                                                                                   │
│                          │ 3. Replace linear CZ chain with parametrized XX/YY gates on all neighbor pairs            │
│                          │ 4. Add a few strategic cross-chain gates for better long-range entanglement               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This reduces distinct parameters from 24 to ~10-12, forcing the circuit to learn more     │
│                          │ generalizable patterns rather than memorizing training data. The parameter sharing acts   │
│                          │ as strong regularization. Parametrized two-qubit gates (XX, YY) replace fixed CZ gates,   │
│                          │ giving us more expressive power per parameter.                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Expected impact:                                                                          │
│                          │ - Reduced overfitting (smaller train-test gap)                                            │
│                          │ - Better worst-group margin (regularization helps hard samples)                           │
│                          │ - Better parameter efficiency                                                             │
│                          │ - Improved combined score through better generalization                                   │
