╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ alternating_block_heterogeneity                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ Drawing inspiration from the recommendation to break strict 3-repeat symmetry and the     │
│                          │ observation that Gen 3 improved by relaxing symmetry, I propose a novel approach that     │
│                          │ defines two distinct ansatz block variants (A and B) that are applied in an A–B–A         │
│                          │ sequence rather than repeating the same block three times.                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key insights from the context:                                                            │
│                          │ 1. Gen 2 (score 0.54) achieved better worst_group_margin (0.31) than the current program  │
│                          │ (0.18) by using per-pair parameters, suggesting expressiveness matters for hard sample    │
│                          │ separation.                                                                               │
│                          │ 2. The current program has a training-test generalization gap of 0.15, indicating         │
│                          │ overfitting despite lower parameter count.                                                │
│                          │ 3. The recommendation explicitly suggests alternating coupling patterns preserve ~12–14   │
│                          │ parameters while introducing heterogeneity.                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Novel approach:                                                                           │
│                          │ - Block variant A uses diagonal ZZ couplings on pairs (0,3) and (4,7)                     │
│                          │ - Block variant B uses diagonal ZZ couplings on pairs (1,4) and (5,0)                     │
│                          │ - Both blocks share the same rotation and single-qubit phase structure (ry_even, ry_odd,  │
│                          │ rz_even, rz_odd)                                                                          │
│                          │ - Nearest-neighbor XX gates appear in both (xx_near)                                      │
│                          │ - Mid-range XX appears in both (xx_far)                                                   │
│                          │ - Block-specific YY cross-couplings differ: A uses (1,3)/(4,6), B uses (2,4)/(5,7)        │
│                          │ - Applied in A–B–A sequence, this creates heterogeneity in the coupling landscape across  │
│                          │ repeats while keeping total distinct parameters ~11–12                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This breaks the dead symmetry that plagues purely homogeneous circuits while maintaining  │
│                          │ parameter economy. The alternating pattern allows the circuit to adapt the input state    │
│                          │ differently at each depth, improving both margin robustness on hard samples and           │
│                          │ generalization to unseen data.                                                            │
