                         Patch Metadata - Gen 20/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ quartet_parity_ansatz_v2                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The current program (score 0.36) suffers from severe underfitting: only 75% train         │
│                          │ accuracy and 52% test accuracy with just 5 distinct parameters. The prior best program    │
│                          │ (score 0.54) achieved 90% test accuracy with 12 distinct parameters through deliberate    │
│                          │ parameter differentiation in RY and RZ layers, combined with targeted entanglement gates. │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight from comparing the three programs:                                        │
│                          │ - **Seed (score 0.51)**: 7 distinct params, 85% test accuracy — heavy sharing in RY/RZ (2 │
│                          │ params each) but rich entanglement (4 two-qubit gates).                                   │
│                          │ - **Gen9 (score 0.54)**: 12 distinct params, 90% test accuracy — per-pair RY/RZ sharing   │
│                          │ (4+4 params) with structured entanglement.                                                │
│                          │ - **Current (score 0.36)**: 5 distinct params, 52% test accuracy — oversimplified with    │
│                          │ global sharing (`ry_shared`, `rz_shared`) loses expressivity.                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The current program fails because it collapses all RY rotations to one parameter and all  │
│                          │ RZ rotations to one parameter, destroying the qubit-specific learning capacity needed for │
│                          │ fine-grained state discrimination.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I propose **quartet_parity_ansatz_v2**: refine the parity-sharing strategy from Gen9 into │
│                          │ a balanced quartet structure:                                                             │
│                          │ - **RY layer**: 4 distinct parameters (`ry_01`, `ry_23`, `ry_45`, `ry_67`), each shared   │
│                          │ within adjacent pairs.                                                                    │
│                          │ - **RZ layer**: 4 distinct parameters (`rz_01`, `rz_23`, `rz_45`, `rz_67`), mirroring RY. │
│                          │ - **Entanglement**: Reduce from Gen9's 4 two-qubit gate types (XX, YY, ZZ chains) to a    │
│                          │ focused 3-gate core:                                                                      │
│                          │   - XX gates on nearest-neighbor pairs (4 pairs, 1 shared param): dense local coupling.   │
│                          │   - YY gates on staggered pairs (3 pairs, 1 shared param): moderate-range correlation.    │
│                          │   - ZZ gates on alternating distance-2 pairs (4 pairs, 1 shared param): long-range        │
│                          │ binding.                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Total: **4 + 4 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 (readout) = 13 distinct parameters** — one fewer than Gen9 │
│                          │ but with better expressivity through balanced quartet sharing. This avoids the            │
│                          │ underfitting collapse of the current design while maintaining parameter economy.          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The quartet structure preserves qubit locality (adjacent pairs share parameters) without  │
│                          │ global sharing, enabling the circuit to learn different rotations for different qubit     │
│                          │ pairs while still achieving significant parameter reduction compared to per-qubit         │
│                          │ freedom.                                                                                  │
