                         Patch Metadata - Gen 36/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 2/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ cyclic_entangler_rotation_via_spec                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current program achieves excellent test accuracy (0.96) but the worst_group_margin    │
│                          │ (0.32) lags validation_margin (0.64), indicating that hardest samples are not being       │
│                          │ separated robustly enough.                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The recommendation proposes breaking the 3× repetition symmetry by defining three block   │
│                          │ variants that cyclically rotate the two-qubit wiring pattern. Since ANSATZ_SPEC is        │
│                          │ applied identically 3 times with shared parameters, we can encode three distinct logical  │
│                          │ blocks within the spec itself by using parameter names that encode block identity.        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ However, a cleaner approach within EVOLVE-BLOCK constraints is to directly construct      │
│                          │ ANSATZ_SPEC as three concatenated blocks with rotated entanglement but shared parameter   │
│                          │ names. This way:                                                                          │
│                          │ - Block 0: entanglement at wires (0,1), (2,3), (4,5), (6,7)                               │
│                          │ - Block 1: entanglement at wires (1,2), (3,4), (5,6), (7,0) — rotated by +1               │
│                          │ - Block 2: entanglement at wires (2,3), (4,5), (6,7), (0,1) — rotated by +2               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ All three blocks share the same parameter names, so when applied by `apply_ansatz_block`  │
│                          │ (which is called 3 times), they will reuse the same learned angles but with different     │
│                          │ spatial connectivity. This adds implicit regularization.                                  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0804                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
│ model_name               │ openrouter/anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5                                                     │
│ temperature              │ 1.0                                                                                       │
│ max_output_tokens        │ 16384                                                                                     │
│ diff_summary             │ added: 21; deleted: 0; modified: 15;                                                      │
